# Project Knowledge

## Purpose

A file-conversion HTTP API (CSV/JSON/XML/YAML, PNG/JPEG/SVG) behind a web frontend: accounts,
e-mail confirmation, DB-driven RBAC, conversion history and saved results, mail and file storage. One deployable NestJS app (modular monolith) talking to one PostgreSQL database. The
OpenAPI contract (`openapi/openapi.yaml`, written first) is what the frontend builds against.

## Tech stack

| Concern | Choice |
| --- | --- |
| Runtime | Node.js 24 LTS (≥ 22.12 for `require(esm)`), npm |
| Framework | NestJS **11** (CommonJS, Jest, ESLint as generated by `@nestjs/cli@11`), TypeScript strict. Not 12: `nestjs-zod` peers stop at 11 |
| Database | PostgreSQL |
| ORM | Prisma 7 **or** TypeORM — one of `07-database-prisma` / `07-database-typeorm` is deleted at setup |
| Transactions | `@nestjs-cls/transactional` with the adapter for the chosen ORM |
| Validation / DTOs | zod via `nestjs-zod` (`createZodDto`, global `ZodValidationPipe`) |
| API contract | hand-written `openapi/openapi.yaml` (OpenAPI 3.1, contract-first), linted by Redocly, enforced by contract tests |
| Config | zod env schema → typed, frozen `AppConfig` (`loadAppConfig()` + dotenv; no `@nestjs/config`) |
| Auth | Cookie JWT access + refresh tokens (refresh not stored server-side), CSRF double-submit, argon2id, DB-driven RBAC |
| Rate limiting | `@nestjs/throttler` |
| Mail | nodemailer over SMTP, sent through the outbox |
| Files | `StoragePort` with `s3` / `local` drivers |
| Tests | Jest + supertest; e2e against a real Postgres |
| Lint / format | ESLint (typescript-eslint strict-type-checked, perfectionist, `no-restricted-imports` layer rules), Prettier |
| Local services | docker compose: Postgres 18, Mailpit, SeaweedFS (S3 API on `:8333`) |

## Principles

- **The contract is explicit.** Every request and response is in `openapi/openapi.yaml` first and a zod DTO second; every
  error is `{ code, detail, context }` with a code from one `ErrorCode` enum.
- **Boundaries are hard.** `config ← common ← infrastructure ← modules`; inside a module
  `Controller → Service → Repository → ORM`. Modules meet only through exported services.
- **The database is the source of truth.** Constraints, indexes and transactions in Postgres, not
  in hopeful application code. Schema changes only through safe migrations.
- **Secure by default.** Every route authenticated unless `@Public()`; ownership checked in the
  service; secrets never logged or returned.
- **Side effects are durable.** Mail and external calls go through the outbox, never inline in a
  request.
- **Boring over clever.** No generic base classes, no framework-on-a-framework, no speculative
  abstraction. Match the closest existing module.

